Below is a **complete, self‑contained console program** that demonstrates how to use `System.Text.Json` for serializing and deserializing a C# object.  
It shows:

* Creating a model class (`Person`) with nested objects and an enum.  
* Configuring `JsonSerializerOptions` (camel‑case naming, indented output, ignoring null values, and enum‑string conversion).  
* Serializing an instance to a JSON string.  
* Deserializing that JSON string back into an object.

```csharp
// Program.cs
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization;

namespace JsonSerializationDemo
{
    public class Program
    {
        public static void Main()
        {
            // Build a sample object to serialize
            var person = new Person
            {
                FirstName = "John",
                LastName  = "Doe",
                Age       = 30,
                Email     = null,                       // Will be omitted because of the option
                Hobbies   = new List<string> { "Reading", "Traveling" },
                Status    = PersonStatus.Active,
                Address   = new Address
                {
                    Street  = "123 Main St",
                    City    = "Anytown",
                    Country = "USA"
                }
            };

            // Configure serializer options
            var options = new JsonSerializerOptions
            {
                WriteIndented          = true,                          // Pretty‑print
                PropertyNamingPolicy   = JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase,    // camelCase JSON keys
                DefaultIgnoreCondition = JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull, // Skip nulls
                Converters =
                {
                    // Convert enums to/from their string names (camelCase)
                    new JsonStringEnumConverter(JsonNamingPolicy.CamelCase)
                }
            };

            // 1. Serialize to JSON
            string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(person, options);
            Console.WriteLine("Serialized JSON:\n");
            Console.WriteLine(json);

            // 2. Deserialize back to a C# object
            Person deserialized = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Person>(json, options);

            // Show the deserialized data
            Console.WriteLine("\nDeserialized Person:");
            Console.WriteLine($"Name: {deserialized.FirstName} {deserialized.LastName}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Age: {deserialized.Age}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Status: {deserialized.Status}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Address: {deserialized.Address.Street}, {deserialized.Address.City}, {deserialized.Address.Country}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Hobbies: {string.Join(", ", deserialized.Hobbies)}");
        }
    }

    // Sample data model
    public class Person
    {
        public string FirstName { get; set; }
        public string LastName  { get; set; }
        public int    Age       { get; set; }
        public string Email     { get; set; }
        public List<string> Hobbies { get; set; }
        public PersonStatus Status { get; set; }
        public Address Address { get; set; }
    }

    public enum PersonStatus
    {
        Active,
        Inactive,
        Pending
    }

    public class Address
    {
        public string Street  { get; set; }
        public string City    { get; set; }
        public string Country { get; set; }
    }
}
```

### How to run

1. Create a new console project (e.g., `dotnet new console -n JsonSerializationDemo`).
2. Replace the generated `Program.cs` with the code above.
3. Build and run: `dotnet run`.

You should see a nicely formatted JSON string in the console, followed by the details of the deserialized `Person` object. This demonstrates the core workflow of JSON serialization and deserialization using `System.Text.Json`.
